\title{The default build takes the young and old session tables, the probe budget of 2^17, the loader's second pass and the repeat skip; the closed-pair table, huge-page advice and a larger session cap stay out}
\taxon{Decision}
\date{2026-11-25}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{performance}
\tag{benchmark}
\tag{machine}
\tag{loader}
\tag{driver}
\tag{platform}
\tag{soundness}
\meta{status}{implemented}
\meta{created}{2026-11-25}
\meta{completed}{2026-11-25}
\import{base-macros}

\p{\strong{Status:} accepted and implemented. The changes are in the main tree and in the published jars since \commit{34e8647}, 20:00 UTC on 2026-11-25 (15:00 local time); the shared executable built from them at that hour is the default build.}

\p{\strong{Context.} [The first wave](dec-JT59) had left the pass of the executable bound by the total CPU of the 8 cores, with RLVM needing 1.6 times gruszka's CPU in the image and 1.3 times on the JVM launcher. Seven agents took one lever each during 2026-11-25, in private copies of the tree, under the rule of [the decision on the default set](dec-YP0N): on the eight corpora the geometric mean of the warm passes goes down and no corpus is more than 3 \% slower, on the executable and on the JVM launcher, with the gates of the default configuration unchanged. Between the two waves [the level fix](log-MVGK) was published; it is not part of this decision, and the build before this wave has it. An integrator merged what was to go in, gated the sum before and after the publication and measured it against the build before the wave and against gruszka: [the report](rep-INT9).}

\p{\strong{Decision.}}
\ul{
\li{\strong{In the default build:} [a young table of fixed size and an old table that grows](log-5HTT) for each of the machine's three session tables ([rep-OPT-D](rep-OPT-D)); [a default budget of 2^17 ticks](log-I3WM) for a probe of eager reduction, where it was 2^22 ([rep-OPT-I](rep-OPT-I), change 1); [the loader thread's second pass](log-0H34), stretches of app, lam and forallE records and a small table in front of the name index ([rep-OPT-F](rep-OPT-F)); [the repeat skip](log-YGJO): a definition, theorem or opaque that repeats an accepted earlier one word for word is admitted and not checked again by the machine (rep-OPT-I, change 2); [the flag pair](log-7WDX) with which the JVM launcher starts on a stock OpenJDK 25 ([rep-OPT-J](rep-OPT-J)); and, in the hostile suite of the machine, [the 78 exports](log-T6DI) that were written against the closed-pair table.}
\li{\strong{One verdict changes with the repeat skip, and it is taken under the practical policy.} The hostile file \code{\verb~|R-e1-sib-krule-major-redex-bad~} of rep-OPT-I is a row of the tolerated kind (finding E1-F7: the official checker rejects a derivable statement through the K rule on a major premise that is a beta-redex) with its theorem repeated under another name. The machine accepts the original through its tabling of the proof block, a tolerated accept, and rejects the copy of the same two records when it checks it, so the default configuration rejected the file at the repeat: the official verdict. With the skip the repeat is admitted on the word of its original and the file is accepted. No underivable statement is accepted by this, on this file or in general: the statement of a repeat is the statement of its original, and the official checker itself gave a repeat the verdict of its original on 1,193 of 1,193 hostile files. Under \code{\verb~|-X policy=strict~} the file is rejected as officially, and \code{\verb~|-X repeats=check~} restores the old verdict. It is the only verdict that differs on the 1,648 hostile files made for the question, it is the same in every schedule, and it is recorded with [the accepted risks](accepted-risks).}
\li{\strong{Not in the default build, and not in the main tree: the closed-pair table} ([rep-OPT-H](rep-OPT-H)), a table of comparison results for closed values that survives the machine's sessions. It is gated with its switch off and on and removes 4.5 to 15.1 \% of the machine's ticks with 8 workers, but it gives no time that the guard resolves on navier-stokes-euler, cslib and Mathlib (CPU of a pass 0.996, 1.002 and 0.993 of the base's), and a table that outlives a session needs the adversarial pass of [the rule for a default](dec-QN1O), which was not made. Its hostile exports are adopted because they hold for the default set without the table.}
\li{\strong{Not in the default build: huge-page advice for the executable's heap} ([rep-OPT-G](rep-OPT-G)). It is worth 2 to 14 \% of a warm pass while the kernel has free blocks of 2 MiB and costs 10 to 50 \% when it has none, because an advised first touch then stalls in the kernel's compaction; the arena's runner has 16 GB for an input of 6 GB. The mechanism was not adopted, see [the insight](ins-EQAW).}
\li{\strong{Not changed: the session cap of 1 MiB.} With the young and old tables a larger cap is slower at every size, 1.5 \% at 2 MiB to 8.1 \% at 16 MiB in the geometric mean of the eight warm passes (rep-OPT-D, section 6; [the insight](ins-58I0)).}
\li{\strong{Not taken: table access without its bounds check.} What is left of the image's distance to the JVM launcher in the workers' code is mostly the code of every array access, a bounds check per element ([rep-OPT-A](rep-OPT-A), section 7). Reading the session tables without that check would remove a safety net under a checker whose first promise is that it never accepts a statement that is not derivable; it was not built.}
\li{\strong{Still not in the default build:} the fast and slow splits of rep-OPT-A and the pooled session arrays of [the report on session memory](rep-V1), as [the first wave](dec-JT59) left them; neither was measured again.}
}

\p{\strong{Evidence: the guard of the published build}, against the build before the wave (the first wave and the level fix). Warm wall clock of a pass that reloads the file with pipelined loading, 8 workers, a run without options; the executable and the JVM launcher with the machine's heap line; aarch64 development host, the 8 measurement cores, one quiet-core window per corpus with the variants interleaved round by round, three rounds, gruszka in the same window; no other job ran on the host. A cell is the mean over the rounds of the median later pass of one process, for a pass under 0.5 s the mean of the later passes; peak RSS is the largest of the three rounds.}

\table{
\tr{\th{Corpus (rounds x passes)}\th{executable: before the wave, s}\th{published, s}\th{published against before}\th{peak RSS before / published, MiB}\th{JVM launcher: before the wave, s}\th{published, s}\th{published against before}\th{peak RSS before / published, MiB}}
\tr{\td{Init (3 x 10)}\td{0.609}\td{0.569}\td{-6.6 \%}\td{384 / 377}\td{0.547}\td{0.523}\td{-4.3 \%}\td{811 / 845}}
\tr{\td{Std (3 x 10)}\td{1.051}\td{0.991}\td{-5.7 \%}\td{609 / 619}\td{0.911}\td{0.846}\td{-7.1 \%}\td{1,463 / 1,444}}
\tr{\td{cedar (3 x 8)}\td{1.479}\td{1.387}\td{-6.2 \%}\td{768 / 765}\td{1.296}\td{1.180}\td{-9.0 \%}\td{1,752 / 1,779}}
\tr{\td{reachability (3 x 15, mean)}\td{0.458}\td{0.421}\td{-8.2 \%}\td{725 / 735}\td{0.343}\td{0.295}\td{-13.9 \%}\td{1,574 / 1,578}}
\tr{\td{con-leche (3 x 6)}\td{1.390}\td{1.277}\td{-8.1 \%}\td{1,347 / 927}\td{1.183}\td{1.095}\td{-7.4 \%}\td{1,787 / 1,770}}
\tr{\td{navier-stokes-euler (3 x 6)}\td{3.452}\td{3.301}\td{-4.4 \%}\td{1,519 / 1,515}\td{3.088}\td{2.961}\td{-4.1 \%}\td{2,297 / 2,287}}
\tr{\td{cslib (3 x 6)}\td{4.784}\td{4.370}\td{-8.7 \%}\td{2,003 / 1,624}\td{4.038}\td{3.696}\td{-8.5 \%}\td{2,818 / 2,431}}
\tr{\td{Mathlib (3 x 4)}\td{13.144}\td{12.292}\td{-6.5 \%}\td{4,420 / 4,062}\td{11.110}\td{10.348}\td{-6.9 \%}\td{5,144 / 4,901}}
\tr{\td{\strong{sum}}\td{26.37}\td{24.61}\td{-6.7 \%}\td{}\td{22.52}\td{20.94}\td{-7.0 \%}\td{}}
\tr{\td{\strong{geometric mean}}\td{\strong{1.839}}\td{\strong{1.714}}\td{\strong{-6.8 \%}}\td{}\td{\strong{1.567}}\td{\strong{1.446}}\td{\strong{-7.7 \%}}\td{}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Primary.} The geometric mean of the eight warm passes goes down on both launchers: in the executable from 1.839 to 1.714 s, -6.8 \%, on the JVM launcher from 1.567 to 1.446 s, -7.7 \%.}
\li{\strong{Guard.} Every corpus is faster on both launchers: by 4.4 \% (navier-stokes-euler) to 8.7 \% (cslib) in the executable, by 4.1 \% (navier-stokes-euler) to 13.9 \% (reachability) on the JVM launcher. No window saw the kernel stall for a huge page.}
\li{\strong{Mathlib.} The executable 13.14 to 12.29 s with a peak resident set of 4.0 GiB where it was 4.3 GiB; the JVM launcher 11.11 to 10.35 s.}
\li{\strong{Both waves.} The executable of the release ran in the same windows on the three largest corpora: navier-stokes-euler 3.87 to 3.30 s, cslib 5.31 to 4.37 s, Mathlib 14.52 to 12.29 s, 15 to 18 \% off a warm pass, with 4.0 GiB resident on Mathlib where the release held 7.5 GiB.}
\li{\strong{Against gruszka}, with its arena run line and 8 workers in the same windows, eight runs per corpus. The published executable needs 1.05, 1.12, 0.99, 0.21, 1.19, 1.26, 1.06 and 1.14 times gruszka's wall clock on Init, Std, cedar, reachability, con-leche, navier-stokes-euler, cslib and Mathlib (12.29 against 10.77 s), where the build before the wave needed 1.13, 1.19, 1.06, 0.23, 1.29, 1.32, 1.17 and 1.22. The published JVM launcher is at 0.97, 0.96, 0.85, 0.15, 1.02, 1.13, 0.90 and 0.96 (Mathlib 10.35 s): ahead of gruszka on six corpora, where it was ahead on three. By CPU both launchers are still behind on seven corpora, the executable by 24 to 63 \%, the JVM launcher by 8 to 44 \%.}
\li{\strong{The sum is less than the product of the parts.} One by one, each against its own base and mostly with the other cores of the host busy, the four changes had given 0.957, 0.976, 0.986 and 0.986 of the base's geometric mean in the executable and 0.912, 0.981, 1.000 and 0.993 on the JVM launcher, products of 0.908 and 0.888; together, on a quiet host, 0.932 and 0.923.}
\li{\strong{The level fix alone} costs nothing that a window resolves: the executable of the first wave without it against the same sources with it, Std 0.999, cslib 0.999, Mathlib 1.002.}
}

\p{\strong{Gates}, every oracle an official verdict; the full record is the report. Before the publication, on the merged private build: the suites of the six touched modules; the store identical to the main tree's in 28 of 28 reader configurations; the gate of the default configuration through the executable and through the jars - 230 of 230 published tests, 17,775 mutants with no soundness and no completeness difference (agree 15,741, declined 2,034), the hostile suite of 1,274 rows with no wrong accept and no wrong reject (agree 1,192, 78 tolerated accepts, 4 declined); the eight corpora accepted with 8 and 4 workers on both launchers, and under the strict policy with no unit declined and no repeat left unchecked; and every counter of a deterministic run attributed: with the probe budget and the repeat skip switched off no counter differs from the main tree, so the session tables and the loader's second pass are cost only, and the two other changes change exactly what their report predicts. On the published build: the same gate through the jars; the gates of the reference engine by name and of the strict policy; the driver's invariance suite on 15,183 files with no wrong verdict and no difference between schedules, and on the 1,648 hostile files with repeats with the six known differences of three rows of the tolerated kind under a one-byte session cap and no other; the machine's own gate with the table audit on; the differential engine on the eight corpora, where the reference engine accepts each of 1,579,800 units, the 136,567 repeats that were not checked among them; the arena's own procedure on its 270 tests; and the JVM launcher on a stock OpenJDK with the same status on all 270.}

\p{\strong{Alternatives considered.}}
\ul{
\li{\em{The larger table sizes alone, without the young and old tables.} Two changed lines instead of a new structure, and rep-OPT-D had seen Init 2 to 7 \% slower with the tables than with the sizes in one window. Measured again before the set was fixed, six interleaved rounds on a quiet host with a control: Init is the same with both (591.5 ms each, control 591.3), reachability needs the tables (0.890 of the passes with the sizes alone) and so does con-leche (0.983). The tables went in.}
\li{\em{Check a repeat whenever its original is still in flight, and leave it at that.} On the one kind of input where the machine's answer for the copy is not its answer for the original, the status of the repeat would then depend on the schedule. The rule that was adopted waits for the original when such a check does not accept (rep-OPT-I, section 3.4); it did not occur on any corpus.}
\li{\em{Keep the repeat skip out because of the one row.} The row is hostile input of a kind that the practical policy already tolerates, the accepted statement is the one its original states, and the strict policy is untouched; against it stand 5 to 12 \% of the units of every corpus that need no second check. Taken; the maintainer can revert it with one setting.}
\li{\em{A probe budget of 2^16 or of 2^18 ticks.} 2^17 lies in the gap of the probe sizes, 2.06 times above the largest probe that completes and 3.5 times below the smallest it cuts (rep-OPT-I, section 2.2; [the three sizes](ins-N4ES)); 2^16 is only 3 \% above the largest probe that completes, so a change of the machine that moves a tick count could cut probes that pay; 2^18 cuts the same five probes after twice the ticks.}
\li{\em{Adopt the closed-pair switch switched off}, so that it can be developed in the main tree. Its report shows that the patch computes what the base computes with the switch off; it was left out all the same: without a gain in time there is nothing to weigh against a table that outlives the session and has had no adversarial pass.}
\li{\em{Adopt each change on its own evidence.} As in the first wave, each had been measured against a base of its own, most of them on a busy host and before the level fix. Two of them meet in one source file, and two change which engine decides a unit; the sum was gated and measured.}
}

\p{\strong{Consequences.} [The benchmark tables](rep-benchmarks) and the table of the root README carry the rows of the published build. The notes that described one table per kind, a budget of 2^22, a driver that checks every unit and a JVM launcher that needs the GraalVM JDK were revised, see [the session](ses-Q8QP). What the two waves leave open, the remaining distance to gruszka first, is in [the problem tree](prob-HC9J).}
